          "prompt": "What are the correct next commands?",
          "situation": "A developer edits two files and wants to commit only the app.py change with a clear message.",
          "title": "Staging the commit",
          "why": "Git's staging area (index) is where you assemble the next commit: git add stages specific files (or hunks with -p), git commit snapshots exactly what's staged. This two-step design lets one working tree produce focused commits \u2014 commit app.py now, notes.txt later. Skipping the stage (commit -a) is a shortcut, not the model."
        },
        {
          "answer": 0,
          "choices": [
            "Add the patterns to .gitignore (*.log, .env, node_modules/) and untrack anything already committed \u2014 gitignore prevents future tracking; it doesn't untrack the past",
