          "prompt": "How do you recover the commits?",
          "situation": "A hard reset in the wrong direction orphaned three commits.",
          "title": "The reset that went backward",
          "why": "git reflog records every HEAD movement \u2014 the commits still exist as objects; only the branch pointer moved. git reset --hard def5678 (or branch recovery <hash>) restores the pointer. Objects linger until gc prunes them (default 90 days for reflog entries). The lesson: --hard destroys working-tree state but history recovery is routine \u2014 reflog is the safety net."
