  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "Git & DevOps Foundations \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Git Core",
          "items": [
            "Staging area \u2014 git's staging area (index) is where you assemble the next commit: git add stages specific files (or hunks with -p), git commit snapshots exactly what's staged.",
            "Working tree \u2014 git diff = working tree vs index (unstaged changes); git diff --staged (aka --cached) = index vs HEAD (what the commit will contain); git diff main...feature = feature's unique changes vs merge-base.",
            "Common trap \u2014 git stash push -m \"wip\", then pull/rebase, then git stash pop.",
            "Common trap \u2014 git diff only shows committed changes.",
            "Common trap \u2014 git rebase -i to rewrite any published commit freely.",
            "Common trap \u2014 edit the old commit's files directly."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Branching & Merging",
          "items": [
            "Branches \u2014 merge shared branches (main, release) to preserve true history \u2014 rebasing those rewrites commits others built on. Stale branches accumulate into a confusing forest; deleting after merge is hygiene.",
            "Isolation \u2014 git switch -c (or checkout -b) creates a branch \u2014 a 41-byte pointer, essentially free \u2014 so the feature develops in isolation: main stays deployable, the feature's history stays reviewable, and integration happens deliberately via merge/PR.",
            "Merge conflicts \u2014 --no-ff forces a merge commit even when a fast-forward is possible, preserving the feature as a named unit with its integration point \u2014 git log --graph shows the branch's lifespan. The commits themselves remain reachable through the merge commit.",
            "Common trap \u2014 rebase for private/local history (linear, reviewable); merge for shared/public history (preserves true chronology).",
            "Common trap \u2014 merge the whole feature branch into main.",
            "Common trap \u2014 conflicts resolve themselves on the next pull.",
            "Common trap \u2014 pick one side with git checkout --ours for everything.",
            "Conflicts are Git asking a human to decide \u2014 edit the file to the correct result (either side or a combination), remove the markers, git add to mark resolved, then commit to complete the merge. --ours/--theirs auto-picks are for mechanical cases (and dangerous as a default). The merge commit then perm\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Remotes & Collaboration",
          "items": [
            "Upstream tracking \u2014 git remote -v shows the name\u2192URL mapping for fetch and push; git branch -r (or git branch -vv) shows remote-tracking branches and their upstream links.",
            "Fork workflow \u2014 in fork workflows, origin = your fork and upstream = the original \u2014 pushing to the wrong one is the classic confusion.",
            "Pull requests \u2014 then you choose: merge (preserves chronology) or rebase (linear). git pull skips the decision by merging immediately, which surprises people mid-rebase-workflow.",
            "Common trap \u2014 fork the repo, branch, push to the fork, open a PR from the fork to upstream.",
            "Common trap \u2014 branch protection is only for public repos.",
            "Common trap \u2014 git fetch origin, inspect (git log origin/main), then integrate deliberately (merge or rebase).",
            "Common trap \u2014 git log shows the remote branches."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "CI Concepts",
          "items": [
            "Stages \u2014 parallelize within a stage (matrix builds), serialize across stages \u2014 feedback speed is a pipeline design goal.",
            "Principle \u2014 CI makes the verify loop automatic and uniform: every push/PR gets checkout \u2192 install \u2192 lint \u2192 test \u2192 build, with merge blocked on red.",
            "Principle \u2014 manual verification decays under pressure; per-change automation catches breakage at the smallest diff.",
            "Common trap \u2014 upload the built artifact in the build job, download it in the deploy job.",
            "Common trap \u2014 split into jobs/stages (lint+unit fast, integration slower, build last) with dependencies.",
            "Common trap \u2014 cache dependency directories (keyed by the lockfile hash).",
            "Common trap \u2014 three separate workflows, one per version.",
            "CI secret stores inject values as masked environment variables \u2014 never in the repo, never in logs, scoped per environment. Hardcoded secrets leak to every clone and log; echoed secrets leak to log storage. Rule: absolute: secrets enter through the platform's secret mechanism, and PRs from forks don't get secrets at all (\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Jenkins Basics",
          "items": [
            "Jenkinsfile \u2014 a declarative Jenkinsfile in the repo makes the pipeline code: versioned, reviewed, diffable, and reproducible per branch (multibranch pipelines build the Jenkinsfile on each branch).",
            "Pipeline as code \u2014 post is the pipeline's finally clause \u2014 deterministic handling of every outcome.",
            "Agents \u2014 Agents (nodes) execute builds; the controller orchestrates.",
            "Controller isolation \u2014 running builds on the controller risks stability (a runaway build kills Jenkins) and security (builds access the controller's filesystem/credentials).",
            "Credentials binding \u2014 the credentials store + credentials() binding injects secrets as masked env vars at runtime \u2014 the Jenkinsfile references only the credential ID.",
            "Secrets \u2014 hardcoded secrets leak to every repo clone and build log.",
            "Common trap \u2014 use a multibranch pipeline: Jenkins discovers branches and PRs, building each one's Jenkinsfile automatically.",
            "Common trap \u2014 shared libraries are only for scripted pipelines.",
            "Common trap \u2014 use agents (nodes): the controller orchestrates, agents execute.",
            "Common trap \u2014 put credentials in the Jenkinsfile as plain strings."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Release Flow",
          "items": [
            "Trunk-based \u2014 Trunk-based (short-lived branches <1 day, merge to main, feature flags for incomplete work) fits continuous delivery: main is always releasable, integration is continuous, merge hell never accumulates.",
            "Breaking changes \u2014 conventional Commits (feat:, fix:, feat!:/BREAKING CHANGE:) make history machine-readable: changelog generators group features, fixes, and breaking changes automatically, with links to commits/PRs.",
            "Branching \u2014 Branching from main would drag in unreleased v2.5 work; committing to main alone leaves production unfixed.",
            "Release branches \u2014 hotfix branches fork from the release tag (the code that's actually live), get the minimal fix, tag the patch release (v2.4.1), and merge back into both main and the release line so the fix isn't lost.",
            "Common trap \u2014 generate from conventional commits (feat:/fix:).",
            "Common trap \u2014 everyone commits to main directly with no review.",
            "Common trap \u2014 gitFlow with long-lived develop/release branches.",
            "Common trap \u2014 wait for the next scheduled release.",
            "Feature flags decouple deploy from release \u2014 the code merges continuously (no merge hell, always integrated and tested) but stays inert until the flag flips. Long branches accumulate conflicts and untested integration risk; big-bang merges are the riskiest integration pattern. Flags also enable gradua\u2026"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "git",
        "def": "Staging area: git's staging area (index) is where you assemble the next commit: git add stages specific files (or hunks with -p), git commit snapshots exactly what's staged."
      },
      {
        "term": "PR",
        "def": "The PR follows its branch: rebase the branch onto current main (resolving conflicts once, linearly), then force-push \u2014 the PR updates in place with the same discussion and reviews. (Force-push to a PR branch is the sanctioned exception t\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Jenkinsfile",
        "def": "Jenkinsfile: a declarative Jenkinsfile in the repo makes the pipeline code: versioned, reviewed, diffable, and reproducible per branch (multibranch pipelines build the Jenkinsfile on each branch)."
      },
      {
        "term": "CI",
        "def": "Principle: CI makes the verify loop automatic and uniform: every push/PR gets checkout \u2192 install \u2192 lint \u2192 test \u2192 build, with merge blocked on red."
      },
      {
        "term": "Stages",
        "def": "Stages: parallelize within a stage (matrix builds), serialize across stages \u2014 feedback speed is a pipeline design goal."
      },
      {
        "term": "Merge",
        "def": "Merge conflicts: --no-ff forces a merge commit even when a fast-forward is possible, preserving the feature as a named unit with its integration point \u2014 git log --graph shows the branch's lifespan. The commits themselves remain reachable\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Branch",
        "def": "Branch protection rules on main: require pull request reviews (with approval counts), require status checks to pass (CI), and block direct pushes (include administrators optionally). Process becomes configuration \u2014 the breakage class dis\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "CHANGE",
        "def": "Breaking changes: conventional Commits (feat:, fix:, feat!:/BREAKING CHANGE:) make history machine-readable: changelog generators group features, fixes, and breaking changes automatically, with links to commits/PRs."
      },
      {
        "term": "SemVer",
        "def": "SemVer: MAJOR for incompatible API changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features, PATCH for fixes. A breaking removal mandates 2.0.0 \u2014 the number is a compatibility contract that dependency ranges (^1.2.3 means <2.0.0) rely on."
      },
      {
        "term": "Branches",
        "def": "Branches: merge shared branches (main, release) to preserve true history \u2014 rebasing those rewrites commits others built on. Stale branches accumulate into a confusing forest; deleting after merge is hygiene."
      },
      {
        "term": "Principle",
        "def": "Principle: CI makes the verify loop automatic and uniform: every push/PR gets checkout \u2192 install \u2192 lint \u2192 test \u2192 build, with merge blocked on red."
      },
      {
        "term": "BREAKING CHANGE",
        "def": "Breaking changes: conventional Commits (feat:, fix:, feat!:/BREAKING CHANGE:) make history machine-readable: changelog generators group features, fixes, and breaking changes automatically, with links to commits/PRs."
      },
      {
        "term": "BFG",
        "def": "Two steps: (1) .gitignore with patterns (*.log, .env, node_modules/, plus !exceptions) stops future tracking; (2) git rm --cached .env removes the already-tracked file from the index while keeping the working copy. gitignore alone never\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "GPG",
        "def": "Annotated tags (-a, with message and optional GPG signature) are immutable pointers to the release commit \u2014 'what shipped' is answerable forever, and CI can build releases from tags. The forge Release (GitHub/GitLab) attaches notes and b\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Release",
        "def": "Annotated tags (-a, with message and optional GPG signature) are immutable pointers to the release commit \u2014 'what shipped' is answerable forever, and CI can build releases from tags. The forge Release (GitHub/GitLab) attaches notes and b\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "MAJOR",
        "def": "SemVer: MAJOR for incompatible API changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features, PATCH for fixes. A breaking removal mandates 2.0.0 \u2014 the number is a compatibility contract that dependency ranges (^1.2.3 means <2.0.0) rely on."
      },
      {
        "term": "Trunk-based",
        "def": "Trunk-based (short-lived branches <1 day, merge to main, feature flags for incomplete work) fits continuous delivery: main is always releasable, integration is continuous, merge hell never accumulates."
      },
      {
        "term": "git pull",
        "def": "`git pull` is fetch + merge in one. What does the first half do alone \u2014 and why would you want the pause between?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Caveat",
        "def": "git cherry-pick replays a commit's diff onto the current branch as a new commit \u2014 surgical porting without merging the whole branch. Standard for hotfixes (fix lands on main, cherry-picked to release branches)."
      },
      {
        "term": "Post",
        "def": "The declarative post block runs conditionally on the build result: always (publish test reports, archive artifacts, clean workspace), failure/unstable (notify), success (promote). Without it, a failed build's evidence dies with the works\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Flags",
        "def": "Feature flags decouple deploy from release: the code merges continuously (no merge hell, always integrated and tested) but stays inert until the flag flips. Long branches accumulate conflicts and untested integration risk; big-bang merge\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Rebase",
        "def": "Rebase rewrites commits. When is rewriting safe \u2014 when you're the only one who's seen them, or after they've been pushed and pulled?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Staging",
        "def": "Staging area: git's staging area (index) is where you assemble the next commit: git add stages specific files (or hunks with -p), git commit snapshots exactly what's staged."
      },
      {
        "term": "Secrets",
        "def": "Secrets: hardcoded secrets leak to every repo clone and build log."
      },
      {
        "term": "Pipeline",
        "def": "Pipeline as code: post is the pipeline's finally clause \u2014 deterministic handling of every outcome."
      }
    ],
